.TH SLAT 1 "September 2026" "slat" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
slat \- terminal multiplexer with tiling panes, tabs and workspaces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B slat
.br
.B slat
.RB [ \-\-help | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B slat
splits one terminal into panes, tabs and workspaces.
The first run starts a background daemon that owns every shell;
.B slat
itself is only a view onto it.
Closing the terminal, or losing an SSH connection, detaches instead of
ending the session, and running
.B slat
again reattaches with every pane as it was.
Attaching from a second terminal takes over from the first.
.PP
Each pane has its own terminal emulator, so full\-screen programs stay
inside their pane, and each keeps a scrollback history that can be
searched.
The session ends when its last shell exits.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short usage summary.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH KEYS
Commands are given by pressing the prefix key, by default
.BR Ctrl\-S ,
followed by one key.
Every other key goes to the program in the active pane.
Press the prefix twice to send it to the program.
Prefix then
.B ?
shows all bindings.
.TP
.BR v ", " h
Split the pane left/right or top/bottom.
.TP
.BR o ", " O
Focus the next or previous pane.
.TP
.RB "arrow keys, " k ", " j ", " H ", " L
Focus the pane in that direction.
.TP
.B m
Move mode: the pane is walked around the layout with
.BR h ", " j ", " k ", " l
or the arrow keys, without pressing the prefix again;
.B q
or Esc leaves. The status bar shows
.BR MOVE .
.TP
.BR < ", " > ", " K ", " J
Move the pane left, right, up or down, swapping it with the neighbour on
that side. Focus follows the pane that moved.
.TP
.BR + ", " \- ", " =
Grow, shrink or equalize panes.
.TP
.B z
Zoom the active pane to full size, or back.
.TP
.B x
Close the pane.
.TP
.BR [ " or PageUp"
Scroll mode: move with
.BR k / j ,
PageUp/PageDown or
.BR g / G ,
search with
.B /
and
.BR ? ,
repeat with
.BR n / N ,
leave with
.B q
or Escape.
.TP
.BR c ", " n ", " p ", " 1 \- 9 ", " , ", " X
New tab, next, previous, go to tab N, rename, close.
.TP
.BR W ", " w ", " P ", " $
New workspace, next, previous, rename.
.TP
.B d
Detach; the session keeps running.
.TP
.B q
Quit and end every shell.
.SH COMMANDS
These talk to a running session over its socket, for scripts and AI agents.
.I PANE
is an id from
.B slat ls
or
.BR active .
.B --json
prints one JSON object. Exit status: 0 ok, 1 error, 2 timed out, 3 the pane is
gone.
.TP
.B slat ls
List every pane with its status: idle (the pane's shell is waiting), working
(a program is running), input (a program is waiting for an answer) or exited.
.TP
.BI "slat status " PANE
Report one pane's status.
.TP
.B slat pane new \fR[\fB--split \fIv\fR|\fIh\fR] [\fB--cwd \fID\fR] [\fB--cmd \fIC\fR] [\fB--target \fIP\fR] [\fB--focus\fR]
Split a pane. Focus stays where it was unless
.B --focus
is given.
.TP
.BI "slat pane close " PANE
Close a pane.
.TP
.B slat send \fIPANE TEXT\fR... [\fB--enter\fR] [\fB--key \fIKEY\fR]
Type into a pane.
.I KEY
is a name such as
.BR enter ,
.BR escape ,
.BR up ,
.B ctrl-c
or
.BR alt-x .
.TP
.BI "slat run " "PANE COMMAND" \fR...
Send a command and press Enter.
.TP
.B slat capture \fIPANE\fR [\fB--lines \fIN\fR] [\fB--history\fR]
Print a pane's text: the visible screen, or the scrollback too.
.TP
.B slat wait \fIPANE\fB --for \fIidle\fR|\fIinput\fR|\fIexit\fR|\fBtext=\fIREGEX\fR [\fB--timeout \fI60s\fR]
Block until the condition holds.
.B --for idle
also returns when the pane stops for input, so a wait never sleeps through a
question.
.SH STATUS BAR
The bar's layout is the
.B [status]
table, not a fixed design:
.PP
.RS
.EX
[status]
left  = " {workspace} | {tabs}"
right = "{badge} pane {pane}/{panes} {time} "
tab   = " {index}:{name}{alert} "
alert = " ?"
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B left
and
.B right
accept
.BR {workspace} ", " {workspace_index} ", " {workspace_count} ", "
.BR {workspaces} ", " {tabs} ", " {tab} ", " {tabs_count} ", " {pane} ", "
.BR {panes} ", " {badge} ", " {time} ", " {seconds} " and " {date} ;
.B tab
accepts
.BR {index} ", " {name} " and " {alert} .
Anything else is literal text, so a Nerd Font glyph can go straight in. Each
placeholder is drawn in the colour that suits it, so there is no colour markup
to write. The right side survives a narrow terminal; the tab list is truncated.
.SH BORDERS
The lines between panes come from the
.B [borders]
table:
.B style
is one of
.IR sharp ,
.IR rounded ,
.IR heavy ,
.IR double ,
.I dashed
or
.IR none ,
and any single glyph can be replaced
.RB ( vertical ", " horizontal ", " top_left ", " top_right ", "
.BR bottom_left ", " bottom_right ", " tee_right ", " tee_left ", "
.BR tee_down ", " tee_up ", " cross ).
Each must be exactly one column wide, or the layout would tear.
.SH ANIMATION
Set by the
.B [animation]
table:
.B split
is how long a new pane takes to appear and
.B move
how long two panes take to trade places (0 turns either off, the maximum is
1s);
.B easing
is
.IR linear ,
.IR out-quad ,
.I out-cubic
or
.IR out-back ;
.B reveal
is
.I curtain
or
.IR none ,
drawn with
.BR glyph .
.B animations
.RI "= " false
turns them all off at once.
.PP
Only the drawing is animated: panes are their final size from the first frame,
so no program in a pane is resized more than once.
.PP
Fonts are the terminal's, not slat's. slat draws characters; which font paints
them is the terminal emulator's business.
.SH THEMES
The status bar, pane borders and overlays take their colours from the
.B [theme]
table:
.PP
.RS
.EX
[theme]
name   = "gruvbox"
accent = "#fabd2f"
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B name
is one of
.IR default ,
.IR gruvbox ,
.IR nord ,
.I rose-pine
or
.IR mono .
The individual colours are
.BR bg ", " fg ", " dim ", " accent ", " border ", " tab_bg ", " tab_fg ", "
.B tab_active_bg
and
.BR tab_active_fg ,
each a colour name, a palette index 0-255, or
.IR #rrggbb .
Programs running in panes keep their own colours.
.SH NOTIFICATIONS
A pane waiting for input marks its tab in the status bar
.RI ( 2:shell\ ? ).
The configuration can also run a command on that change:
.PP
.RS
.EX
[agent]
on_input = 'notify-send "slat: pane %p needs input" %c'
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B %p
is the pane id,
.B %t
its tab,
.B %s
the status and
.B %c
the pane's last line. Values are shell-quoted, so pane output can never become
part of the command. Hooks run detached and their output goes to the daemon log.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/slat/config.toml
Configuration (prefix key, shell, status bar, scrollback length and key
bindings); defaults to
.IR ~/.config/slat/config.toml .
Errors in it are reported when
.B slat
starts.
See
.I /usr/share/doc/slat/config.example.toml
for every setting.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/slat\-UID.sock
The daemon's socket, accessible only to its user; the temporary directory
is used when
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset.
The daemon writes its errors to the
.I .log
file next to it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHELL
The shell started in new panes, unless the configuration sets one.
.TP
.B SLAT
Set inside panes;
.B slat
refuses to start when it is set, to avoid nesting a session in itself.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR screen (1)
.PP
https://github.com/noturbob/slat
